WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.920
Cool.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.010
So yeah, this is basically
what it's going to be,

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.310
just the grid layout.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:13.260
I took the colors, actually,
from the app, the hex colors.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.590
So they're actually
the hex colors.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:17.910
And yeah.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:23.100
Basically, also changing
this input to be down here.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.220
Just make it a little bit closer
to what the actual app is.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:31.300
So we can stop this.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:31.800
Cool.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.490
And so basically, when
going through this,

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.130
seeing the original
app, I always

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.230
use testy for some reason.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.930
Because "test" is
not five letters.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.160
Right?

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.380
Winston built it
with a verbatim text

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.010
output, which is easy and good.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.660
But if we want to
style it, we're

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:57.030
going to start wanting to get
it into HTML dom elements.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.420
And so basically, taking
this result output here.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.840
And instead of having it be
verbatim text output we're

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.325
going to do, uiOutput.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.080
Output.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.590
Call it result also.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:12.090
Comma.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.000
And then, we can get rid of
this verbatim text output.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.750
And so that itself should work.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.260
It just doesn't give you
the-- it's just text.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.080
But that works,
just replacing that.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.260
But what we
eventually want to do

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.740
is, get it into a bunch
of individual divs

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.010
so that we can put a nice
border around each letter,

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.530
color it, do all that stuff.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:42.630
So right now, the
way that it works,

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.490
output result is a renderText.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.020
We're going to need
to change that.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:52.300
And looks like there's a
vapply that makes a character.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.340
So it's basically assuming
it's a character vector.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.650
And then, it pastes
everything together here.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:06.890
So what we are going to do
is, make this not a vapply,

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:11.360
but an lapply so that
it can just be a list.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.910
By doing that, we need
to get rid of this.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:18.120
And then, format_result, just
to make sure it's working.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.460
We can just wrap it in the
div, tags div function.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:24.390
Save that.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.450
We can run it.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.690
But it gives us this output.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:35.470
And that's just because we're
still doing this paste here.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:44.120
So we can get rid of that
by just returning out_str.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:44.870
Reload it.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.680
Now we're getting an error.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.205
That's because, like I said,
we need to change this.

00:02:56.665 --> 00:02:58.040
Because right now,
render text is

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.498
trying to paste
everything together

00:02:59.498 --> 00:03:02.180
because it says I'm
putting out text.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.478
And we are just giving
it a list of divs,

00:03:04.478 --> 00:03:05.645
so that's not going to work.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.360
It's now working.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.000
This is a div.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.126
We can check that,
though, to make sure.

00:03:17.126 --> 00:03:20.890
Just do an in-line style.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:24.418
I like to do outline
as my default,

00:03:24.418 --> 00:03:25.960
check to see if
something is working.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.380
So I do outline
1px solid salmon,

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:33.690
because it's my
favorite CSS color.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.170
And so we see, we are
indeed getting a div.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:41.630
The div has the
whole guess in it.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.120
So we can do "shiny"
as our second guess.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:45.140
So we get two divs.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.723
They're stacking on
top of each other.

00:03:46.723 --> 00:03:48.720
That's good.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.830
Pretty much what we want.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.790
But in here, the
div body is still

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.010
just this text pasted together.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:00.090
So we should update
format_result,

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.800
because div takes
that as the input.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.990
So we need to make this
not return just a string,

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:08.580
but return a list of divs.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:15.960
So we can do this by writing
our loops in a for loop

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.100
over the letters
in a given guess.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.440
And so builds this out_str.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.640
So we're going to replace
out_str with out_divs.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.280
We're going to make that a
taglist, which is basically

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.030
just a fancy list that
works well with the tags,

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:36.520
like div and stuff.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:42.250
And then, in here,
you can say, out_divs.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:47.010
The ith element is going
to be equal to a new div.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:53.090
It is this internal
section here.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.910
So we can pull that out.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.590
Pull that out.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:06.980
Use the braces there.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:14.315
And then, last, this
one just has spaces.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:19.920
Get rid of all of
these out strings.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:27.300
I have just returned
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.710
And so in theory,
this should now--

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:32.210
yep.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.540
So now it's behaving differently
because by default, divs

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.540
themselves like to stack
on top of each other.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.100
They don't go in line.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.758
It's because they're a
block-level CSS element,

00:05:43.758 --> 00:05:45.300
so that basically
says, the next one,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.660
I'm going down instead of over.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:53.510
But one of the things
that we can do here

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:57.470
is, put some style around this.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:01.468
So we could say, instead
of making a bunch of divs,

00:06:01.468 --> 00:06:03.260
we could just do the
[AUDIO OUT] one time--

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.710
bring this down here--

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:09.550
and have this if statement
give us the inside text.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:14.635
So we'd say,
letterText equals that.

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:20.385
And then, just bring
these back to pastes.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:28.400
So the idea here
is to do a single--

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.320
no, well, not that--

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:32.000
do a single div call.

00:06:34.725 --> 00:06:36.850
So that way, we can do
stuff like put a class on it

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:39.017
without having to repeat
that class a million times.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:44.030
So that should be good.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:48.580
We don't need to do
the assignment here,

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.380
these assignments.

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:54.740
So then, once we've assigned
letterText in this--

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:58.600
indent that-- this if
statement assigns letterText

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.618
based on how we
did the result. So

00:07:01.618 --> 00:07:03.160
that we can just
take that letterText

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:06.070
and put it in this
div, just quickly.

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:08.230
Make sure it worked.

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:10.940
OK, looks like it worked.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.575
So then, can do some
in-line styles here.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:22.630
So we'll do, outline
1px solid blanchedalmond

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:24.280
is another good CSS color.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:32.290
OK, so now we can see,
we had this wrapping div.

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:34.090
Each individual
letter within here

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:35.615
is taking up the whole span.

00:07:35.615 --> 00:07:37.240
That's because of
these block elements.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:42.310
So I think a good
way to proceed,

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:45.085
since we want that grid, we want
them to sit next to each other,

00:07:45.085 --> 00:07:48.340
we could just try,
width is 50 pixels.

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:51.820
Height, 50 pixels.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:53.230
Just see what this gives us.

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:02.680
OK, so they're boxes now,
but they're still stacked.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.240
So I guess if we wanted to make
them sit next to each other

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:13.420
like they should, there's a few
different ways we can do this.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.040
I think the way
that I want to do

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:21.060
it, just because I am familiar
with it, is to use CSS grid.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.146
So CSS grid is basically
a way of saying,

00:08:24.146 --> 00:08:28.385
I want you to lay this stuff
out in a different way.

00:08:28.385 --> 00:08:31.530
And you can say, I want to
fill in this many columns

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:32.460
and this many rows.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:33.990
And so this works
really well here,

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.690
because we know we
have five columns.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.870
And so we can start doing that.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.878
But first, we should probably--

00:08:42.878 --> 00:08:45.420
right now, we're building our
styles in these in-line styles.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.060
And that works fine for
just a couple of things.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:53.590
But it's going to get pretty
unwieldy very quickly.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:56.790
So what we can do
is, include some CSS.

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:01.060
And there's a bunch of
different ways to do this.

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:06.090
The easiest way is
to just put the stuff

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:08.520
in the head of the UI.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:10.500
So that's tags$head.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:12.420
Says, anything that
goes within this

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:16.690
gets placed into the head of
the UI HTML that's generated,

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:21.330
which is part of the page
that doesn't actually show up,

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:25.050
but you can put resources like
CSS and JavaScript in there.

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:26.520
Doesn't really matter.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.010
Just know that you
have to put your style.

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:33.230
So you can do
tags.style within that.

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:36.530
And then, from there, there's
one more thing you have

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:40.520
to remember-- and this bites
me every time I do this--

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:43.280
is that you have to wrap this--
because we're going to write it

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.470
in text, in line--

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.210
to wrap it in this
HTML function.

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:50.600
And that just allows
us to use characters

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:57.710
that CSS likes, such as
the less than or greater

00:09:57.710 --> 00:10:01.610
than signs, which would,
by default, get translated

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.220
into weird Unicode
character things.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:06.360
But CSS actually wants them.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:07.970
So we need to wrap
it in HTML, or we

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:10.970
will have very
confusing bugs that will

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:13.710
take a long time to understand.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:16.760
So in theory,
tags$head, tags$style,

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:18.590
now we can just start
writing CSS in there,

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:21.090
and it'll apply to page.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:23.840
So we could test that,
again, by targeting

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:28.760
this result, because the
uiOutput creates a div that

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:30.290
shoves all of your UI into it.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:34.350
And then, the ID of that
is the ID that you give it.

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:36.085
So we can target
that real quick.

00:10:36.085 --> 00:10:43.440
Could say, CSS rule targeting
result. And say, let's see,

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.603
background color.

00:10:45.603 --> 00:10:47.270
Just another-- since
we're already using

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:49.430
outlines, let's use
background color.

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:55.370
And for background color,
we can do light-grey.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:57.410
I believe that's the right way.

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:01.160
So see if this works.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:02.090
Did not.

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:03.950
OK, so background
color light-grey

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.930
is probably not the right one.

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:07.505
Let's just do steelblue.

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:13.110
There we go.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:14.160
OK.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:19.560
So this is div result, which
contains all of our stuff,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:20.200
has background.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:26.440
So we're successfully targeting
it with this CSS rule.

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:31.685
So what we're going to do is, go
back and do this CSS Grid stuff

00:11:31.685 --> 00:11:33.810
so that we can get it to
lay out how we want it to.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:36.120
Because we have each
letter being a div,

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:39.502
we want them to sit,
five next to each other,

00:11:39.502 --> 00:11:40.960
and then wrap down
to the next row.

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:42.780
So for each guess.

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:45.090
So we could do, display, grid.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:48.150
Which is how you say,
OK, this div, within it,

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:51.240
use the grid layout engine.

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.970
And then, we can do,
grid-template-columns.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:58.110
Repeat.

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:00.720
So we're saying, repeat
this thing five times.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.590
5, and 50 pixels.

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:05.130
So that's saying,
make me a grid that

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:10.930
has columns that are defined
as five columns of 50 pixels.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:14.700
This is a syntax that's
kind of hard to understand.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.080
And I know it because I
work with it all the time.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:24.780
But there's a really
good resource,

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:29.400
which is this complete guide
to CSS Grid, which is where

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.485
I go for almost everything.

00:12:31.485 --> 00:12:32.860
It gives you all
sorts of things.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:36.840
So if you [AUDIO OUT] forget
anything with CSS Grid,

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:40.080
this is a fantastic
resource for it.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.920
So going back
here, we're saying,

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:43.980
it's going to be a grid.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:48.180
It's going to have five
columns, each 50 pixels wide.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:51.620
So we can save and reload.

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:54.860
And it's not doing
what we want it to.

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:58.730
But interestingly,
there is a border

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:04.670
around this that is the color
that we put of this div.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:06.300
That's right, it's
the salmon border.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:08.960
And so we actually
don't want that div.

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:11.990
We just want the results
dumped right in there.

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:14.270
So we can just get rid
of that div in general

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:15.710
so that all of our divs--

00:13:19.185 --> 00:13:20.150
yeah, there we go.

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:22.000
So we were wrapping it in a div.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:25.180
And CSS Grid says,
I'm going to--

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:26.620
I don't know about the children.

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:28.240
I only care about
the first child.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:31.240
I don't care about the
children of your children.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:34.390
So the div within
it will be a column.

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:36.850
And then, the next one is
another column, and so on.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:38.620
But right in the way
that we were doing,

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:41.290
it we were wrapping all of
the letters in a single div.

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:44.200
And so it didn't actually know
to put them how we want them.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.240
But now it is.

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:52.480
And so now we see five values
in their little boxes here.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:54.010
We can do another
guess, just to see

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:55.540
if it wraps how we want it to.

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.410
Let's get shiny.

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:57.910
It does.

00:13:57.910 --> 00:13:59.560
So now we have five.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:00.670
New guess down here.

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:02.860
Five.

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.710
And all we're doing is
building these divs.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:10.120
And so what we should do
now is, obviously, this text

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:11.140
is up in the upper left.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:13.860
And that's not great.

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:16.710
So we need to target
these individual divs.

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:21.930
And before, what we were
doing is, this in-line styles.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:23.790
But that's not
great, so we're going

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:27.525
to take that out and bring
it back up to our CSS chunk.

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:34.080
So we can call--

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:35.830
there's a couple of
ways we could do this.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:39.060
We could say-- we could
give this div a class

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:41.820
itself and then refer
to that class up here.

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:48.690
Or we could say,
result, and then, div.

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:51.150
Which is saying, of
all of the divs that

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:54.630
are children of result, this
is the style I want to apply.

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:56.370
But I think that
what we want to do

00:14:56.370 --> 00:14:59.590
is, make a new class, just to
be a little bit more readable.

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:04.932
So we could say, guess-letter.

00:15:04.932 --> 00:15:07.390
Then we're going to paste in
that style that we had before.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:13.710
Save that.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:18.310
We need to go down here
and put the div class in.

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:22.150
But let's run it without
that first, just to see.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:24.870
So yeah, we get, they're
laid out in that order,

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:26.120
but they don't have the style.

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:29.020
So now, in theory, to get that
style back, we should just say,

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:35.670
class equals guess-letter.

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:40.250
So that should give this div
the class of guest-letter.

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:41.548
So that should apply this.

00:15:41.548 --> 00:15:42.465
Let's see if it works.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:46.050
Did.

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:46.590
OK, cool.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:48.870
So now, we want to
style these guys,

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:52.060
we can just do it right
there, which is nice.

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:55.565
So within that, we could
say, first thing, I think,

00:15:55.565 --> 00:15:56.940
we're going to
want to do is, put

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:00.360
this actual letter in
the center of the box

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:02.410
so it's not wasting
all that space.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.010
So there's a bunch
of different ways

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.740
to center something in CSS.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:13.440
It's one of those problems
in computer science,

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:17.550
along with caching,
aligning stuff in CSS.

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:20.160
But the easiest way to
do it that I'm aware of

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:22.200
is to do, display, grid.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:26.580
So say, this child element
also has a display grid.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:29.910
And then, place content, center.

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:33.600
And that basically just
centers everything completely

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:37.430
within the div.

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:40.770
So now, we have everything
perfectly centered.

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:41.780
So that's nice.

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:44.880
So it's starting to look
a little bit better.

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:48.110
One thing that we can do to
make it a little bit more

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:50.570
like the Wordle thing is
have them not butted up right

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:51.990
against each other.

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:56.390
So there's a property
on the grid, CSS Grid.

00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:57.870
It's called gap.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:16:58.790
So we say, gap.

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:01.260
And we'll set it to 5 pixels.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:04.910
So that's basically saying,
place these items of the grid

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:09.060
five pixels away
from each other.

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:09.560
Cool.

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:11.180
So now they have that gap.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:13.829
It's getting better.

00:17:13.829 --> 00:17:15.619
We could round the
edges a little bit

00:17:15.619 --> 00:17:20.480
and say, border-radius.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:22.190
Also say that's 5 pixels.

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:24.420
So that should just say--

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:25.700
I'm going to round the edges.

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:26.794
Oh, but I did it wrong.

00:17:29.650 --> 00:17:30.610
Oh, you know what?

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:33.490
I need to do,
border, not outline.

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:34.645
Border radius, border.

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:39.700
There we go.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.850
Border, on newer
browsers, the outline

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:44.500
will respect this border radius.

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:49.480
But on older browsers, such as
the built-in viewer in RStudio,

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:52.120
you need to use the border.

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:54.730
Which is kind of confusing,
that there's border and outline.

00:17:54.730 --> 00:17:57.730
But, just, there
is at this point.

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:04.135
So now that we have that,
things are looking pretty good.

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:07.810
Stuff is working.

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:10.900
But I think the next
step is going to be--

00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:12.460
right now, we're
using this indicator

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:15.890
of wrapped-in parentheses means
that it's in the wrong place.

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:17.710
But in the word and
then square brackets,

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:20.420
mean that it's correct.

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:23.080
So that's probably
not the best way

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:25.630
